content.text:
Yes — it works, and here's exactly why, tied to how I split the function:

```c
static void node_release_payload(struct rb_node *n, rb_value_free_fn value_free)
{
	free(n->key);
	if (value_free != NULL)
		value_free(n->value);
}   /* <- note: no free(n) here */

static void node_release(struct rb_node *n, rb_value_free_fn value_free)
{
	node_release_payload(n, value_free);
	free(n);   /* <- only this one frees the struct */
}
```

`node_release_payload` **never calls `free(n)`**. It only frees `n->key` and calls `value_free(n->value)`. So `node_release_payload(z, t->value_free)` frees `z`'s *old* key/value and leaves `z` itself as a completely valid, live struct — which is exactly what's needed, since the very next lines do `z->key = s->key; z->value = s->value;`, reassigning fields on a `z` that's still alive and well. The zero/one-child branch uses `node_release` (the version that also does `free(n)`) precisely because `z` really is going away there. That distinction was the whole point of splitting the function in the plan.

**One real thing did surface while implementing it** — not a flaw in the logic, but a compile-order bug: `node_release_payload`/`node_release` were defined *after* `rb_delete` in the file, so calling them from `rb_delete` failed with "use of undeclared identifier." Fixed by moving the pair up next to `node_alloc` (their natural pair — matches the companion's own suggested module map), which also fits this file's existing convention of defining every helper before its first use.

Verified: `make test` (all cases, 100k fuzz ops) and `make asan` both clean. No behavior changed, only where the duplication lives.

